    <note class="self-check">
      <para>Sophia is making parts for a model ship. The number of parts, \( p \), she can make in a day varies directly with the number of hours, \( h \), she works. She can make 28 parts in 4 hours. How many parts would Sophia make in 9 hours?</para>
    </note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section>
        <title>Solve Application Problems Using Direct Variation</title>
        <para>In applications using direct variation, generally we will know values of one pair of the variables and will be asked to find the equation that relates \( x \) and \( y \). Then we can use that equation to find values of \( y \) for other values of \( x \).</para>
        <para>Here’s how to solve applications of direct variation problems.</para>
        <note>
          <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation for direct variation. Identify the variables.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the given values for the variables.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for the constant of variation.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation that relates \(x\) and \(y\) using the constant of variation.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the value for the variable.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Solve.</para>
        </note>
        <para>Let’s look at an application problem:</para>
        <para>If the cost of a pizza varies directly with its diameter, and an 8-inch pizza costs $12, how much would a 6-inch pizza cost?</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation for direct variation. Identify the variables. \( y = kx \)</para>
        <para>\(y\): cost of pizza, \(x\): diameter</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the given values for the variables. \(12 = k(8)\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for the constant of variation. \(k= \frac32 \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation that relates x and y using the constant of variation. \( y= \frac{3}{2}x \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the value for the variable. \( y= \frac{3}{2}(6) \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Solve. \( y = 9 \)</para>
        <para>What does this mean in the context of the problem?</para>
        <para>A pizza that has a diameter of 6 inches costs $9. </para>
        <note class="try">
          <title> Try It: Solve Application Problems Using Direct Variation</title>
            <para>The number of buckets of water, \(b\), filled by a spring varies directly with the number of minutes, \(m\), passed. The spring fills 3 buckets of water in 14 minutes.</para>
              <exercise>
            <problem>
              <para>How many buckets would be filled in 42 minutes?</para>
              <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution>
              <para>Compare your answer: Here is how to model this application problem of direct variation.</para>
              <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation for direct variation. Identify the variables. \( y = kx \) \( b=km \)</para>
              <para>\(b\): number of buckets, \(m\): number of minutes</para>
              <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the given values for the variables. \( 3=k(14) \)</para>
              <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for the constant of variation. \( k= 3/14 \)</para>
              <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation that relates \(m\) and \(b\) using the constant of variation. \( b= \frac{3}{14}m \)</para>
              <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the value for the variable. \( b= \frac{3}{14}(42) \)</para>
              <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Solve. \(b= 9\) buckets</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
